/** \page faq FAQ

\htmlonly

    <div class="contents">
        <h3>Can I write closed source applications using Pt?</h3>
        <p>
            Yes, you can. Pt is distributed under the LGPL with two license
            exceptions that also allow static linking and incorporation of
            inline code from the Pt header files (i.e. C++ class templates).
        </p>

        <br/
>
        <h3>Why is Pt licensed under the LGPL with exceptions?</h3>
        <p>
            The LGPL requires that users must be able to replace the LGPL
            code with a modified version. This is impossible for C++ code,
            where large parts consist of inline functions and templates. This
            requirement renders the LGPL equivalent to the GPL.
        </p>
        <p>
            It also complicates static linking, because the object files of
            the program that links against a static version of the library
            would have to be made available, so the user can relink the
            application.
        </p>
        <p>
            The special exception permits convenient use of the library in
            proprietary and non-GPL open source applications.
        </p>

    </div>

\endhtmlonly

*/
